 "output_text": "{\n  \"claim_id\": \"c5\",\n  \"stance_summary\": \"The claim is supported. First-principles calculations demonstrate that incorporating four-phonon scattering significantly lowers the predicted lattice thermal conductivity of boron arsenide (BAs), for instance reducing the room-temperature prediction from approximately 2200 to 1400 W/m K.\",\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\n      \"quote\": \"For the projected ultrahigh-thermal conductivity material, zinc-blende BAs, a competitor of diamond as a heat sink material, four-phonon scattering is found to be strikingly strong as three-phonon processes have an extremely limited phase space for scattering. The four-phonon scattering reduces the predicted thermal conductivity from 2200 to 1400 W/m K at room temperature.\",\n      \"url\": \"https://journals.aps.org/prb/abstract/10.1103/PhysRevB.96.161201\",\n      \"source_label\": \"Physical Review B (Feng, Lindsay, and Ruan)\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"confidence\": 0.95\n}",
